  "content": {
    "text": "If market history is any guide, then a fresh tech sell-off isn't over by any stretch of the imagination.\nTech stocks were battered on Friday amid a one-two punch of events. For one, a stronger-than-expected May jobs report ratcheted up fears of a Federal Reserve rate hike. That prompted profit-taking in hot areas of the market, namely momentum tech stocks such as Micron (MU), Marvell (MRVL), and SanDisk (SNDK).\nCompounding this was a disappointing quarterly outlook from Broadcom (AVGO) that failed to raise full-year custom AI chip targets late last week.\nWhile dip-buyer surfaced in tech on Monday, BTIG chief technical strategist Jonathan Krinsky wrote in a note that it's buyer beware.\nLast Friday marked the 22nd time the Nasdaq Composite has lost 4% or more (on a Friday) in the last 30 years, per Krinsky. In 17 of the prior 21 occurrences, the Nasdaq broke Friday's intraday low on the following Monday. 19 of 21 times it broke Friday's low by the following Friday.\nOnly twice did it take longer than a week, but in every case, the low was tested eventually.\n\"If history is any guide, when the Nasdaq Composite loses 4% or more on a Friday there is a 90% chance that Friday low is breached in the next five trading days, and we don't think this time is different,\" Krinsky wrote. \"This is often a spot to lighten up if you were feeling some pain on Friday.\"\nThere is a small silver lining to Krinsky's data set, provided one has a longer-term investment horizon.\nAfter all 4% plus Friday sell-offs on the Nasdaq dating back to 1999, the index was up by an average of 3.19% in the 10 days after. It's up by 4.15% in the 20 days after.\nBrian Sozzi is Yahoo Finance's Executive Editor and a member of Yahoo Finance's editorial leadership team. Follow Sozzi on X @BrianSozzi, Instagram, and LinkedIn. Tips on stories? Email brian.sozzi@yahoofinance.com.\nClick here for in-depth analysis of the latest stock market news and events moving stock prices\nRead the latest financial and business news from Yahoo Finance",
    "text_blocks": [
      "If market history is any guide, then a fresh tech sell-off isn't over by any stretch of the imagination.",
      "Tech stocks were battered on Friday amid a one-two punch of events. For one, a stronger-than-expected May jobs report ratcheted up fears of a Federal Reserve rate hike. That prompted profit-taking in hot areas of the market, namely momentum tech stocks such as Micron (MU), Marvell (MRVL), and SanDisk (SNDK).",
      "Compounding this was a disappointing quarterly outlook from Broadcom (AVGO) that failed to raise full-year custom AI chip targets late last week.",
      "While dip-buyer surfaced in tech on Monday, BTIG chief technical strategist Jonathan Krinsky wrote in a note that it's buyer beware.",
      "Last Friday marked the 22nd time the Nasdaq Composite has lost 4% or more (on a Friday) in the last 30 years, per Krinsky. In 17 of the prior 21 occurrences, the Nasdaq broke Friday's intraday low on the following Monday. 19 of 21 times it broke Friday's low by the following Friday.",
      "Only twice did it take longer than a week, but in every case, the low was tested eventually.",
      "\"If history is any guide, when the Nasdaq Composite loses 4% or more on a Friday there is a 90% chance that Friday low is breached in the next five trading days, and we don't think this time is different,\" Krinsky wrote. \"This is often a spot to lighten up if you were feeling some pain on Friday.\"",
      "There is a small silver lining to Krinsky's data set, provided one has a longer-term investment horizon.",
      "After all 4% plus Friday sell-offs on the Nasdaq dating back to 1999, the index was up by an average of 3.19% in the 10 days after. It's up by 4.15% in the 20 days after.",
      "Brian Sozzi is Yahoo Finance's Executive Editor and a member of Yahoo Finance's editorial leadership team. Follow Sozzi on X @BrianSozzi, Instagram, and LinkedIn. Tips on stories? Email brian.sozzi@yahoofinance.com.",
